4. FY2026 MID-YEAR BUDGET AMENDMENT
   Ms. Marsh presented the mid-year amendment. Chemical costs are 11% over
   budget (sodium hydroxide unit price increase). Staff proposed covering the
   overage from the rate stabilization reserve.

   4a. Intake PS-1 valve replacement - closeout. Under budget by $18,400.

   4b. OT SECURITY IMPROVEMENTS (staff request: $186,000)
   Ms. Whitfield and Mr. Pruitt presented recommendations based on the
   CISA/EPA water-sector guidance, including CISA advisory AA23-335A
   (attacks on Unitronics PLCs using default passwords, Nov. 2023) and the
   2021 incident at Oldsmar, Florida, where a remote user raised the sodium
   hydroxide setpoint at that city's treatment plant. Requested items:
     - Change default/shared passwords on PLCs and the HMI; unique accounts
       for control room operators ............................ $ 14,000
     - Replace the shared vendor remote-access account with
       individual, MFA-protected, time-limited access ......... $ 38,000
     - Firewall rule review / cleanup between the business
       network and the SCADA network ......................... $ 22,000
     - Third-party OT security assessment ................... $ 65,000
     - PLC/RTU firmware updates (Hydra Controls) ............ $ 47,000
   Director Ibarra asked whether any of the items were mandatory. Mr.
   Lindqvist said they are "strongly recommended" but not currently required
   by the state. Director Kowalczyk noted the Ashby member assessment would
   rise 2.3% if funded mid-year. Mr. Kessler stated the plant has never had a
   cyber incident and the SCADA network is "not on the internet."

   MOTION (Kowalczyk / Freeman) to approve the mid-year amendment EXCLUDING
   item 4b, and to DEFER the OT security improvements to the FY27 budget
   process, with staff to pursue DWSRF/state cybersecurity grant funding.
   Carried 4-1 (Ibarra opposed).

